## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end API testing in an event-driven pipeline, focusing on idempotency, schema versioning across tenants, and concurrency safety — realistic in multi-tenant ingestion workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent ingestion and deduplication across HTTP and Kafka
- Tenant-scoped schema versions and overrides
- End-to-end visibility with distributed tracing
- Race conditions and replay handling in high concurrency
- Tooling: Testcontainers, Pact, k6, Postman

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: idempotent ingestion using idempotency-key header
async function testIdempotentIngest(client) {
  const payload = {tenant: 't1', version: 2, data: {a:1}};
  const key = 'abc-123';
  await client.post('/ingest', payload, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  await client.post('/ingest', payload, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  // verify only single consume
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect exactly-once vs at-least-once processing in the consumer?
- How would you test schema evolution across tenants without breaking data?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's ability to test asynchronous, event-driven systems with idempotency, resilience patterns, and cross-service contracts. It assesses understanding of production-grade reliability and distributed systems testing.

## Key Concepts

- Asynchronous job processing and polling patterns
- Idempotency keys and deduplication strategies
- Dead-letter queues (DLQs) for failed processing
- Retry mechanisms with exponential backoff
- Schema evolution in event-driven architectures (Kafka)
- Contract testing and observability

## Code Example

```java
@Test
void testIdempotency() {
    String idempotencyKey = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

    Response first = given()
        .contentType("application/json")
        .body(jobPayload)
        .header("Idempotency-Key", idempotencyKey)
        .post("/jobs");

    Response second = given()
        .contentType("application/json")
        .body(jobPayload)
        .header("Idempotency-Key", idempotencyKey)
        .post("/jobs");

    assertEquals(first.jsonPath().getString("jobId"), 
                 second.jsonPath().getString("jobId"));
}

@Test
void testRetryWithBackoff() {
    String jobId = submitJob();

    await().atMost(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
           .until(() -> getStatus(jobId).equals("DONE"));

    verify(retries, times(3));
    verify(backoff, times(2)); // exponential: 1s, 2s
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This angle tests robustness of an event-driven payments pipeline, focusing on idempotency, end-to-end correctness, and resilience under partial failures. It mirrors real-world fintech and high‑throughput marketplaces where retries and outbox patterns are essential.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys and exactly-once semantics in asynchronous flows
- Outbox pattern, event bus (Kafka), and compensating actions
- Fault injection, timeouts, retries, and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: spawn N concurrent requests with varying idempotency keys
const axios=require('axios');
async function send(batch) {
  const requests=batch.map((i)=>axios.post('/payments/process', {
    amount:100, currency:'USD', paymentMethod:'card', idempotencyKey:`k-${i}`
  }));
  return Promise.all(requests);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure idempotency effectiveness under network partitions?
- How would you simulate non-deterministic outbox flush delays across regions?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes multi-service integration testing, data freshness guarantees, and resilience under partial downstream failures, which are common in finance-grade APIs with merged views.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end correctness for merged data from multiple backends
- Eventual consistency and freshness windows across services
- Fault injection, latency scenarios, and canary testing
- Observability: tracing, metrics, and test data management

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo test sketch for freshness of merged transactions
async function testMergedTransactionsFreshness(accountId, freshnessMs) {
  const res = await httpGet(`/accounts/${accountId}/transactions`);
  const now = Date.now();
  for (const tx of res.transactions) {
    if (now - tx.lastUpdated > freshnessMs) throw new Error("stale data");
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize this approach to N downstream services with different freshness guarantees?
- How would you quantify impact on SLOs when latency spikes or partial outages occur?

